---
/**
 * Privacy notice. It describes what the code actually does, and the text adapts to the
 * configuration: analytics appear only if PUBLIC_GA4_ID is set. The processors named below
 * (Cloudflare for hosting and email delivery, Google for the inbox) must match the production
 * setup (ENQUIRY_DELIVERY=cloudflare); update this page if that ever changes.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PreviewNote from '../components/PreviewNote.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/site';
import { PUBLIC_GA4_ID, PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY } from 'astro:env/client';

const updated = '1 November 2026';
const contactEmail = profile.contact.email;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What information this website collects through the project brief form, why, where it goes, how long it is kept, and your choices."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="label">Privacy</p>
      <h1>How your information is handled</h1>
      <p class="muted">Last updated {updated}.</p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The only personal information this site collects is what you type into the project brief form.</li>
        <li>I use it only to reply to you and, if we work together, to run the project.</li>
        <li>I don’t sell it, rent it or add you to a mailing list.</li>
        <li>
          {PUBLIC_GA4_ID
            ? 'Visit statistics are collected only if you allow them, and never include what you type into forms.'
            : 'Visits are counted without cookies, and there are no advertising or tracking cookies.'}
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the brief form collects</h2>
      <p>
        Your name and email (required); optionally your business name, website, phone number, budget range and
        timeframe; the services you tick; and your message. The server also uses your IP address briefly, in memory, to
        limit repeated submissions, and keeps only a one-way hash of it, in memory, for that purpose. Like most websites, the hosting provider’s standard logs may also record IP addresses for security and troubleshooting. Hidden fields record a time
        check and a random submission number, used to block spam and prevent duplicate messages.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it goes</h2>
      <p>
        When you send the form, the website’s server checks it and emails it to my inbox. Two services handle it on my
        behalf, only to do that job:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts this website, runs the code that checks the form, and delivers the email
          with your brief (Cloudflare Email). Emails to {contactEmail ?? 'my business address'} pass through Cloudflare
          Email Routing too.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Google</strong> (Gmail) hosts the inbox where those emails arrive.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both companies are based in the United States and run their networks worldwide. Your information may be
        processed and stored outside Canada, including in the United States, where it is subject to local laws.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you call, text or message me on WhatsApp, your number and messages are handled by your phone carrier or by
        WhatsApp (Meta) under their own terms. I use them only to reply to you.
      </p>
      {
        PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY && (
          <p>
            The form uses Cloudflare Turnstile to check that a person, not a bot, is sending it. Cloudflare processes
            technical information about your browser for that check.
          </p>
        )
      }

      <h2>How long it’s kept</h2>
      <p>
        Enquiries that don’t become projects are deleted from my inbox within 12 months. If we work together, project
        records are kept for as long as needed for the work and for tax and legal requirements. Cloudflare keeps
        limited email delivery logs under its own policy.
      </p>

      <h2 id="analytics">Analytics and cookies</h2>
      <p>
        Cloudflare, which hosts this site, counts page views with Cloudflare Web Analytics. It doesn’t use cookies or
        browser storage, doesn’t build a profile of you across websites, and never sees what you type into the form.
      </p>
      {
        PUBLIC_GA4_ID ? (
          <p>
            If you choose “Allow”, Google Analytics 4 records usage statistics, such as pages viewed and whether a brief
            was sent (counted as an event, without its contents), using a cookie that recognises your browser but not
            your name or email. Advertising features are off. Your choice is stored in
            your browser’s local storage; clear it to be asked again. If you choose “No thanks”, Analytics doesn’t load.
          </p>
        ) : (
          <p>
            There are no advertising or tracking cookies, and nothing here asks for your consent because nothing needs
            it. If that changes, this page will be updated and you will be asked before anything loads.
          </p>
        )
      }

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <p>
        You can ask to see, correct or delete the information you sent. Canadian privacy law (PIPEDA) gives you these
        rights.
        {contactEmail ? (
          <>
            {' '}Email <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
          </>
        ) : (
          ' Reply to any email from me, or send a brief saying what you need.'
        )}
      </p>
      {!contactEmail && <PreviewNote note="a privacy contact email is recommended" />}

      <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
      <p>
        Links to other websites, including Nest &amp; Rest Cleaning, have their own privacy notices.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: calc(var(--container-text) + 2 * var(--gutter));
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-s);
  }
  h1 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.3em;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }
</style>
